WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.952
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:07.380 --> 00:00:09.090
GOPAL ASHOK: So the whole--

00:00:09.090 --> 00:00:11.310
if you just look at the
application ecosystem today,

00:00:11.310 --> 00:00:12.810
I think it's very
fair for us to say

00:00:12.810 --> 00:00:14.850
that there are some
fundamental characteristics

00:00:14.850 --> 00:00:18.180
that the applications
that we build today have.

00:00:18.180 --> 00:00:19.890
Regardless of whether
you're building

00:00:19.890 --> 00:00:23.870
a departmental application or
an internet scale application,

00:00:23.870 --> 00:00:26.700
the speed and the response
time for applications

00:00:26.700 --> 00:00:30.280
are super important
in today's world.

00:00:30.280 --> 00:00:32.259
You couple that with
scale, you couple that,

00:00:32.259 --> 00:00:34.310
and you add high availability.

00:00:34.310 --> 00:00:40.300
And you add the need to run
application infrastructure

00:00:40.300 --> 00:00:41.780
at a certain operational cost.

00:00:41.780 --> 00:00:44.840
It becomes a very
interesting problem.

00:00:44.840 --> 00:00:46.300
So when it comes
to caching, why is

00:00:46.300 --> 00:00:49.150
caching such a
fundamental infrastructure

00:00:49.150 --> 00:00:51.370
when it comes to applications?

00:00:51.370 --> 00:00:53.470
Caching is not a new concept.

00:00:53.470 --> 00:00:54.820
Everybody uses caching.

00:00:54.820 --> 00:00:56.710
From our perspective,
the use cases

00:00:56.710 --> 00:00:59.740
that we see when we see
customers use caching,

00:00:59.740 --> 00:01:02.990
primarily is around two
high level use cases.

00:01:02.990 --> 00:01:05.890
One is the obvious
one, which is latency.

00:01:05.890 --> 00:01:08.290
You want to reduce latency
of the application.

00:01:08.290 --> 00:01:10.390
You want to have an
in memory caching

00:01:10.390 --> 00:01:12.040
layer in between
your application

00:01:12.040 --> 00:01:14.650
layer and your database layer.

00:01:14.650 --> 00:01:17.030
That's the main use case.

00:01:17.030 --> 00:01:19.030
The other one that we
also see customers use

00:01:19.030 --> 00:01:20.500
is to reduce cost.

00:01:20.500 --> 00:01:22.990
So you can argue that if you
have a back end database where

00:01:22.990 --> 00:01:27.400
you are charged at 40 ops per
second, or the number of ops

00:01:27.400 --> 00:01:31.600
that you are using for the back
end, a caching layer in between

00:01:31.600 --> 00:01:33.100
can actually help
you reduce cost.

00:01:33.100 --> 00:01:35.020
So that's one of the
other use cases that we

00:01:35.020 --> 00:01:37.870
see customers use, or the
benefit that customers

00:01:37.870 --> 00:01:40.100
try to get out of caching.

00:01:40.100 --> 00:01:44.410
But the second use case
or that second benefit

00:01:44.410 --> 00:01:45.920
is kind of tricky
because you have

00:01:45.920 --> 00:01:49.220
to be very careful in terms
of how you slice your cache

00:01:49.220 --> 00:01:51.630
and not get into this whole
notion of a Thundering Herd

00:01:51.630 --> 00:01:52.130
problem.

00:01:52.130 --> 00:01:53.870
If part of the
cache goes down, do

00:01:53.870 --> 00:01:57.032
you have enough capacity
on the back end, et cetera.

00:01:57.032 --> 00:01:58.490
So that's a specific
case where you

00:01:58.490 --> 00:02:00.323
want to be super careful
in terms of how you

00:02:00.323 --> 00:02:01.742
design your caching and system.

00:02:01.742 --> 00:02:03.200
But when you look
at the use cases,

00:02:03.200 --> 00:02:04.760
and there's a whole
bunch of them,

00:02:04.760 --> 00:02:07.610
I mean literally across all
different types of applications

00:02:07.610 --> 00:02:08.660
caching is used.

00:02:08.660 --> 00:02:11.760
And I don't think we need
to elaborate on that.

00:02:11.760 --> 00:02:13.760
But the whole idea
is that you have

00:02:13.760 --> 00:02:15.530
a cache in your
application structure

00:02:15.530 --> 00:02:18.230
so that the data
access layer can

00:02:18.230 --> 00:02:21.170
get that frequently accessed
data from memory rather

00:02:21.170 --> 00:02:21.965
than from disk.

00:02:21.965 --> 00:02:23.840
But there's a whole
bunch of things behind it

00:02:23.840 --> 00:02:25.710
in terms of how to
optimally build a cache.

00:02:25.710 --> 00:02:28.550
And we'll talk more about that.

00:02:28.550 --> 00:02:32.025
From a caching
pattern perspective,

00:02:32.025 --> 00:02:34.150
I would say the read through
cache is fundamentally

00:02:34.150 --> 00:02:37.180
what everybody uses mostly.

00:02:37.180 --> 00:02:39.600
Which essentially
says that, hey, go

00:02:39.600 --> 00:02:41.610
try to get the data from cache.

00:02:41.610 --> 00:02:43.610
If it's not there, go
read it from the database.

00:02:43.610 --> 00:02:46.150
And once you read from
the database, cache it,

00:02:46.150 --> 00:02:48.698
so that subsequent queries can
get the data from the cache.

00:02:48.698 --> 00:02:50.740
So that's kind of the
fundamental caching pattern

00:02:50.740 --> 00:02:53.440
that most applications use.

00:02:53.440 --> 00:02:55.970
But then we also see write
through caching patterns.

00:02:55.970 --> 00:02:57.950
How many of you use
write through today?

00:02:57.950 --> 00:03:01.660
Or any implementation
of write through?

00:03:01.660 --> 00:03:03.360
So write through is
an interesting case

00:03:03.360 --> 00:03:06.510
where it is supposed to
solve a lot of the problems

00:03:06.510 --> 00:03:07.680
around cache consistency.

00:03:07.680 --> 00:03:13.220
But it is a complex logic and
there are issues that comes,

00:03:13.220 --> 00:03:15.000
or consistency issues
that can crop up.

00:03:15.000 --> 00:03:16.625
And there are a lot
of error conditions

00:03:16.625 --> 00:03:18.157
that you need to watch out for.

00:03:18.157 --> 00:03:19.740
So essentially write
through a caching

00:03:19.740 --> 00:03:22.690
means you write to
the cache first,

00:03:22.690 --> 00:03:24.480
and then write to the
back end database.

00:03:24.480 --> 00:03:29.730
And you only commit once the
writes are in both the places.

00:03:29.730 --> 00:03:32.370
And write behind essentially,
which is very, very rarely used

00:03:32.370 --> 00:03:34.360
means you just write
to the cache first,

00:03:34.360 --> 00:03:36.870
and then you lazily write
into the back end database.

00:03:36.870 --> 00:03:39.000
So that's an eventual
consistency model.

00:03:39.000 --> 00:03:41.600
And any eventual
consistency model

00:03:41.600 --> 00:03:43.790
it really depends
on your application.

00:03:43.790 --> 00:03:47.145
So if your application can
tolerate that kind of a model,

00:03:47.145 --> 00:03:49.090
great.

00:03:49.090 --> 00:03:51.780
But the other one that we
also see is in process.

00:03:51.780 --> 00:03:54.340
So what I mean by in
process is that if you want

00:03:54.340 --> 00:03:57.100
to have an application close
to you, or a cache close

00:03:57.100 --> 00:04:00.320
to your application node
for example, you can have,

00:04:00.320 --> 00:04:03.550
for example, a Redis
process running directly

00:04:03.550 --> 00:04:07.120
in your application node
to cache like local data.

00:04:07.120 --> 00:04:09.340
So the reason I bring
that up in this context

00:04:09.340 --> 00:04:13.060
is because sometimes when you
have a managed service that

00:04:13.060 --> 00:04:16.180
gives you a managed Redis,
which is kind of a distributed

00:04:16.180 --> 00:04:18.880
centralized cache, the in
process caching use case

00:04:18.880 --> 00:04:21.519
you may be better off just
deploying your own Redis along

00:04:21.519 --> 00:04:23.530
with, for example,
if you're running GKE

00:04:23.530 --> 00:04:26.320
and if you are running your
application node in GKE,

00:04:26.320 --> 00:04:29.380
it may be better off just
running Redis directly

00:04:29.380 --> 00:04:31.890
in the node rather than using
a centralized management

00:04:31.890 --> 00:04:32.740
managed cache.

00:04:32.740 --> 00:04:35.530
But these are some of the
different varying caching

00:04:35.530 --> 00:04:38.980
patterns that we see.

00:04:38.980 --> 00:04:40.150
So why Redis?

00:04:40.150 --> 00:04:41.650
I think everybody
knows about Redis.

00:04:41.650 --> 00:04:43.870
But I think just to
quickly summarize,

00:04:43.870 --> 00:04:48.430
we all know Redis is a very
versatile in-memory data store.

00:04:48.430 --> 00:04:50.110
It is categorized
as key value store.

00:04:50.110 --> 00:04:52.480
But there are so many
different uses of it

00:04:52.480 --> 00:04:57.590
that I feel it is more than
just a key value store.

00:04:57.590 --> 00:05:01.410
But if you look at the
key capabilities of Redis,

00:05:01.410 --> 00:05:03.660
from-- just forget
about the latency part,

00:05:03.660 --> 00:05:04.783
which is pretty obvious.

00:05:04.783 --> 00:05:07.200
But the data structure is one
of the most powerful aspects

00:05:07.200 --> 00:05:07.700
of Redis.

00:05:07.700 --> 00:05:09.450
Like there are all
kinds of different data

00:05:09.450 --> 00:05:11.580
structures that allows
you to efficiently process

00:05:11.580 --> 00:05:13.230
data for various use cases.

00:05:13.230 --> 00:05:15.180
And I'll touch upon
that a little bit.

00:05:15.180 --> 00:05:17.638
But the other things like high
availability and persistence

00:05:17.638 --> 00:05:23.250
also make it a lot more usable
for different use cases.

00:05:23.250 --> 00:05:25.530
And I'll talk about these
characteristics and the use

00:05:25.530 --> 00:05:28.660
cases in just a bit.

00:05:28.660 --> 00:05:31.740
There's capabilities like
Pub/Sub, Scripting, Modules,

00:05:31.740 --> 00:05:33.810
and now Streams in
Redis 5.0, which

00:05:33.810 --> 00:05:37.590
is pretty powerful when it comes
to processing streaming data.

00:05:40.875 --> 00:05:42.250
And when we talk
about use cases,

00:05:42.250 --> 00:05:44.458
obviously there are all
kinds of different use cases.

00:05:44.458 --> 00:05:47.650
The very common ones that
we see at least in what we

00:05:47.650 --> 00:05:50.680
have seen with memory store
is obviously the first,

00:05:50.680 --> 00:05:53.930
I would say four,
are pretty common.

00:05:53.930 --> 00:05:57.020
So caching, straight
up query caching.

00:05:57.020 --> 00:05:59.860
Session store is very,
very common with Redis.

00:05:59.860 --> 00:06:01.240
So essentially
you basically want

00:06:01.240 --> 00:06:05.320
to have your session
stored in Redis.

00:06:05.320 --> 00:06:10.980
And rate limiting, we have a
lot of customers basically using

00:06:10.980 --> 00:06:13.530
readies Redis for
API rate limiting.

00:06:13.530 --> 00:06:16.300
That's one of the common
use cases that we see.

00:06:16.300 --> 00:06:18.060
Fast ingest is an
interesting use case

00:06:18.060 --> 00:06:21.300
where you can use various
data structures within Redis

00:06:21.300 --> 00:06:24.000
to basically quickly
process streaming

00:06:24.000 --> 00:06:30.220
data into your pipeline.

00:06:30.220 --> 00:06:33.420
So when you look at these
use cases, like I said,

00:06:33.420 --> 00:06:34.920
there are different
data structures

00:06:34.920 --> 00:06:37.260
that you can use to basically
support these use cases.

00:06:37.260 --> 00:06:39.510
I think most of you are
probably very familiar with it

00:06:39.510 --> 00:06:40.760
if you're already using Redis.

00:06:40.760 --> 00:06:42.540
I'm just listening a few.

00:06:42.540 --> 00:06:44.760
Session store, like
for example, HASH

00:06:44.760 --> 00:06:49.350
is a very interesting
data structure in Redis.

00:06:49.350 --> 00:06:51.330
HASH essentially allows
you to basically take

00:06:51.330 --> 00:06:52.890
a set of key values
and basically

00:06:52.890 --> 00:06:54.890
put them in a higher
level data structure.

00:06:54.890 --> 00:06:56.950
And it's like very
memory efficient.

00:06:56.950 --> 00:06:58.770
So one of the things
that we recommend

00:06:58.770 --> 00:07:03.810
is if you are using essentially
just plain key value,

00:07:03.810 --> 00:07:06.282
it's probably a lot
of key value pairs,

00:07:06.282 --> 00:07:07.740
it's probably better
to take a look

00:07:07.740 --> 00:07:10.200
and see whether you can
convert that into HASHes,

00:07:10.200 --> 00:07:12.830
because it's very
memory efficient.

00:07:12.830 --> 00:07:15.110
Sorted set is also
very powerful.

00:07:15.110 --> 00:07:18.840
Sorted set, essentially, it's
a mapping of member's scores

00:07:18.840 --> 00:07:21.660
that allows you to easily
sort through the scores

00:07:21.660 --> 00:07:23.485
and basically give
you, hey, what is

00:07:23.485 --> 00:07:27.220
a top ten for example, player.

00:07:27.220 --> 00:07:30.305
Or the top 10 players
in the game, et cetera.

00:07:30.305 --> 00:07:31.930
So I'm not going to
go deep into those.

00:07:31.930 --> 00:07:33.972
I just want to give you
a quick overview in terms

00:07:33.972 --> 00:07:35.920
of why Redis is so popular.

00:07:35.920 --> 00:07:39.570
And it looks like a lot of
you are already using Redis.

00:07:39.570 --> 00:07:43.200
So the interesting
thing about it,

00:07:43.200 --> 00:07:45.960
when you move to the cloud,
one of the key benefits

00:07:45.960 --> 00:07:47.760
that everybody wants
to get is that you

00:07:47.760 --> 00:07:49.740
don't want to manage your own
application infrastructure.

00:07:49.740 --> 00:07:51.615
That is fundamentally
one of the key benefits

00:07:51.615 --> 00:07:53.760
that everybody's looking for.

00:07:53.760 --> 00:07:55.530
So from a database
portfolio perspective,

00:07:55.530 --> 00:07:58.180
you probably will see the
slide through many sessions.

00:07:58.180 --> 00:08:02.490
But the point is that we
offer a variety of databases,

00:08:02.490 --> 00:08:04.740
as managed services.

00:08:04.740 --> 00:08:09.270
So Cloud Memorystore is our
managed in-memory data store.

00:08:09.270 --> 00:08:11.160
And it's essentially
managed Redis.

00:08:11.160 --> 00:08:15.150
And I'll talk more about Cloud
Memorystore going forward.

00:08:15.150 --> 00:08:18.290
And we also have the
Non-relational databases

00:08:18.290 --> 00:08:21.227
of Cloud Bigtable, which is our
wide column store, obviously

00:08:21.227 --> 00:08:23.310
Cloud Spanner, which is
our distributed relational

00:08:23.310 --> 00:08:26.670
database, BigQuery, which
is our data warehouse.

00:08:26.670 --> 00:08:29.632
And the other thing, if
you listen to the keynote,

00:08:29.632 --> 00:08:31.590
one of the things that
we are also trying to do

00:08:31.590 --> 00:08:33.570
is to work very closely
with our partners

00:08:33.570 --> 00:08:36.480
and make sure that if there are
capabilities that the partner

00:08:36.480 --> 00:08:38.730
ecosystem provides,
you can easily

00:08:38.730 --> 00:08:43.575
get that from Google Cloud
in a very integrated manner.

00:08:43.575 --> 00:08:46.200
So the bottom line here is that
there's a portfolio of services

00:08:46.200 --> 00:08:48.420
that we provide and
the key goal for us

00:08:48.420 --> 00:08:50.160
is to provide it as
a managed service

00:08:50.160 --> 00:08:52.830
so that you don't
have to do the work

00:08:52.830 --> 00:08:55.930
in terms of managing
these infrastructure.

00:08:55.930 --> 00:08:58.330
All right, so let's dive into
the core of the talk which

00:08:58.330 --> 00:09:00.210
is Cloud Memorystore.

00:09:00.210 --> 00:09:02.430
This a high level I would
say marketing slide.

00:09:02.430 --> 00:09:04.288
But the key point
here is that there

00:09:04.288 --> 00:09:06.330
are three things that we
try to optimize for when

00:09:06.330 --> 00:09:07.730
we run this managed service.

00:09:07.730 --> 00:09:10.410
One is, it's going to
be fully managed by us.

00:09:10.410 --> 00:09:12.690
So what that means is
that any kind of patching,

00:09:12.690 --> 00:09:15.510
security vulnerabilities
that come from Redis,

00:09:15.510 --> 00:09:18.190
et cetera, we
automatically take care of.

00:09:18.190 --> 00:09:22.230
We probably have already done
two security patches already

00:09:22.230 --> 00:09:23.200
that comes from Redis.

00:09:23.200 --> 00:09:25.720
We get notified even
before you get to know.

00:09:25.720 --> 00:09:28.435
So we take care of all that.

00:09:28.435 --> 00:09:30.060
The other thing that
we really focus on

00:09:30.060 --> 00:09:33.840
is this availability and
reliability of the service.

00:09:33.840 --> 00:09:35.580
For me as a product
manager, I keep

00:09:35.580 --> 00:09:38.610
telling my team that reliability
is our number one feature.

00:09:38.610 --> 00:09:40.260
And because that
is fundamentally

00:09:40.260 --> 00:09:45.550
what you expect us to do from
a managed service perspective.

00:09:45.550 --> 00:09:47.310
So when it comes to
reliability, I'll

00:09:47.310 --> 00:09:48.810
talk about the
different offerings

00:09:48.810 --> 00:09:51.930
that we have and the
kind of capabilities

00:09:51.930 --> 00:09:54.510
that we provide with
Cloud Memorystore.

00:09:54.510 --> 00:09:56.710
But the key thing is
that we use replication.

00:09:56.710 --> 00:10:00.710
We have automatic Failover and
we also provide SLAs around it.

00:10:00.710 --> 00:10:03.510
And the last thing,
last but not the least,

00:10:03.510 --> 00:10:08.180
from a security perspective,
we have various capabilities

00:10:08.180 --> 00:10:10.940
that we provide to make sure
that the system is secure.

00:10:10.940 --> 00:10:14.880
And I'll talk more about
the details of that.

00:10:14.880 --> 00:10:18.620
So Memorystore, if you're
not using Memorystore,

00:10:18.620 --> 00:10:20.340
Memorystore comes
in two flavors.

00:10:20.340 --> 00:10:22.520
One it's what we
call the basic tier.

00:10:22.520 --> 00:10:26.680
And that's essentially a
standalone instance of Redis.

00:10:26.680 --> 00:10:28.485
So standalone
instance, so if you

00:10:28.485 --> 00:10:29.860
ask the question,
what do you get

00:10:29.860 --> 00:10:31.660
with that standalone
instance, we essentially

00:10:31.660 --> 00:10:33.285
take care of all the
health monitoring.

00:10:33.285 --> 00:10:36.790
We take care of the automatic
recovery from failures.

00:10:36.790 --> 00:10:39.940
The other thing that we also
do is that IP preservation.

00:10:39.940 --> 00:10:43.430
So in case the system goes
down and comes back up.

00:10:43.430 --> 00:10:45.280
You are guaranteed
to have the same IP.

00:10:45.280 --> 00:10:47.290
So from an application
perspective,

00:10:47.290 --> 00:10:50.390
you don't have to
worry about IP changes.

00:10:50.390 --> 00:10:55.580
The standard tier is the
replicated Redis instance.

00:10:55.580 --> 00:10:57.050
What we provide
with standard tier

00:10:57.050 --> 00:11:00.200
is a cross zone
replicated Redis.

00:11:00.200 --> 00:11:03.410
So we have a Redis instance that
is replicated at cross zones.

00:11:03.410 --> 00:11:06.500
We automatically take
care of the failover,

00:11:06.500 --> 00:11:07.830
and it's a single endpoint.

00:11:07.830 --> 00:11:10.190
So we essentially
have a virtual IP that

00:11:10.190 --> 00:11:12.620
is in front of the instance.

00:11:12.620 --> 00:11:16.260
And when failover happens
or the node back and forth,

00:11:16.260 --> 00:11:18.590
you're essentially
connecting to a single IP.

00:11:18.590 --> 00:11:22.410
So there's no IP change
when that happens.

00:11:22.410 --> 00:11:25.560
And we allow you to scale
up and down seamlessly.

00:11:25.560 --> 00:11:27.690
And I'll walk through
the demo and show you

00:11:27.690 --> 00:11:30.750
how this works in
actuality, in the demo.

00:11:30.750 --> 00:11:33.600
But we also provide three
nines of availability

00:11:33.600 --> 00:11:35.310
for the standard tier service.

00:11:38.900 --> 00:11:42.770
And the other thing that I want
to kind of quickly touch upon

00:11:42.770 --> 00:11:47.680
is that the way we have exposed
servers, essentially, is that--

00:11:47.680 --> 00:11:50.990
Redis is a single
credit service.

00:11:50.990 --> 00:11:55.430
So what we have essentially
done is behind the scenes,

00:11:55.430 --> 00:11:58.520
we tiered the service, meaning
the more memory you provision,

00:11:58.520 --> 00:12:00.057
you get more network throughput.

00:12:00.057 --> 00:12:01.640
So behind the scenes,
what's happening

00:12:01.640 --> 00:12:03.950
is that we adjust the number
of CPUs, every provision,

00:12:03.950 --> 00:12:06.038
depending on the
size of the instance

00:12:06.038 --> 00:12:07.080
that you're provisioning.

00:12:07.080 --> 00:12:09.800
So the higher the memory,
the higher the throughput.

00:12:09.800 --> 00:12:11.703
That's a provisioning model.

00:12:11.703 --> 00:12:14.120
And I'll show that in the demo
in terms of how that works.

00:12:17.070 --> 00:12:22.550
So we went GA about
six months back.

00:12:22.550 --> 00:12:24.950
And we've been doing a lot
of work behind the scenes

00:12:24.950 --> 00:12:28.950
to provide more features.

00:12:28.950 --> 00:12:31.910
So we have Redis 4.0
currently in beta.

00:12:31.910 --> 00:12:34.520
We are working on 5.0 too.

00:12:34.520 --> 00:12:38.420
The other thing that we
are enabling as of next

00:12:38.420 --> 00:12:41.300
is the ability to
access Memorystore

00:12:41.300 --> 00:12:43.400
from App Engine Standard,
2nd generation runtimes.

00:12:43.400 --> 00:12:45.710
How many of you use
App Engine at all?

00:12:48.340 --> 00:12:49.720
Does this matter?

00:12:49.720 --> 00:12:52.580
Or is it useful?

00:12:52.580 --> 00:12:55.470
Yeah, so again, the point
that I want to make here

00:12:55.470 --> 00:12:57.180
is that it's only
for 2nd gen runtime.

00:12:57.180 --> 00:13:00.150
So if you're using App Engine
Standard 1st generation

00:13:00.150 --> 00:13:03.450
runtimes, obviously that
only supports App Engine SDK.

00:13:03.450 --> 00:13:05.430
Memorystore releases
both Redis' protocols.

00:13:05.430 --> 00:13:06.900
So if you're using
2nd gen runtime,

00:13:06.900 --> 00:13:13.018
you can essentially
connect to Memorystore.

00:13:13.018 --> 00:13:15.060
The other thing that we've
been asked quite a bit

00:13:15.060 --> 00:13:18.480
from customers is the ability
to test your application

00:13:18.480 --> 00:13:20.240
before you deploy,
especially when you're

00:13:20.240 --> 00:13:21.490
using standard tier instances.

00:13:21.490 --> 00:13:23.698
So one of the things that
you want to be able to test

00:13:23.698 --> 00:13:27.350
is the failover, the behavior
when the service fails.

00:13:27.350 --> 00:13:30.360
So we have exposed the
manual failover command

00:13:30.360 --> 00:13:35.470
that you can use to test your
service before you deploy it.

00:13:35.470 --> 00:13:37.890
We added a bunch of
new monitoring metrics.

00:13:37.890 --> 00:13:39.780
We have learned a
lot since GA in terms

00:13:39.780 --> 00:13:41.670
of how customers are using it.

00:13:41.670 --> 00:13:44.100
So we are exposing
more and more metrics

00:13:44.100 --> 00:13:46.470
to help you understand how
your system is behaving

00:13:46.470 --> 00:13:48.250
and how you want
to react to that.

00:13:48.250 --> 00:13:50.020
So that best practices
section, we'll

00:13:50.020 --> 00:13:52.520
actually walk through each of
these metrics and more of some

00:13:52.520 --> 00:13:55.020
of the things that you should
be monitoring and watching out

00:13:55.020 --> 00:13:56.610
for.

00:13:56.610 --> 00:13:58.940
And lastly, we've been
continuously adding regions.

00:13:58.940 --> 00:14:00.895
We are pretty much
all over the--

00:14:00.895 --> 00:14:02.270
pretty much in
all regions except

00:14:02.270 --> 00:14:04.550
maybe like three or
four, which we probably

00:14:04.550 --> 00:14:06.200
would roll out pretty quickly.

00:14:06.200 --> 00:14:09.430
And then we are continuing
to work on our compliance

00:14:09.430 --> 00:14:13.640
and support for Memorystore.

00:14:13.640 --> 00:14:15.890
So this is probably
interesting for folks

00:14:15.890 --> 00:14:18.440
who haven't used Memorystore.

00:14:18.440 --> 00:14:20.390
So I just want to walk
through the product

00:14:20.390 --> 00:14:23.000
and give you an overview,
but at the same time

00:14:23.000 --> 00:14:27.560
answer some questions that
aren't certain characteristics

00:14:27.560 --> 00:14:28.340
of Memorystore.

00:14:28.340 --> 00:14:29.840
So Memorystore
essentially is listed

00:14:29.840 --> 00:14:34.627
under the storage portfolio
in Google Cloud Console.

00:14:34.627 --> 00:14:35.960
And it's pretty straightforward.

00:14:35.960 --> 00:14:38.710
I think we made it pretty
simple to go and provision

00:14:38.710 --> 00:14:39.640
a Redis instance.

00:14:39.640 --> 00:14:41.994
I'm just going to
create one right now.

00:14:47.790 --> 00:14:50.670
So from a version
perspective, we

00:14:50.670 --> 00:14:53.490
recommend that you
start using 4.0

00:14:53.490 --> 00:14:56.190
if you are going to start
using Memorystore now.

00:14:56.190 --> 00:14:58.620
We support both 3.2 and 4.0.

00:14:58.620 --> 00:15:01.350
We are currently
beta, but we will

00:15:01.350 --> 00:15:04.080
go GA pretty much
in like a few weeks.

00:15:04.080 --> 00:15:07.660
But we highly recommend that you
start using 4.0 going forward.

00:15:07.660 --> 00:15:09.660
And this is where I was
talking about the tiers.

00:15:09.660 --> 00:15:12.540
You can select either
basic or standard tier.

00:15:12.540 --> 00:15:14.460
Just going to select
standard tier.

00:15:14.460 --> 00:15:18.115
So what happens in the standard
tier essentially is, Redis--

00:15:18.115 --> 00:15:19.740
Memorystore is a
regional service, what

00:15:19.740 --> 00:15:20.910
we call a regional service.

00:15:20.910 --> 00:15:24.542
So you can pick any region
that you want to deploy to.

00:15:24.542 --> 00:15:27.000
So as you can see, we're pretty
much in most of the regions

00:15:27.000 --> 00:15:27.500
today.

00:15:30.420 --> 00:15:35.070
And the zone essentially
you can leave it at any.

00:15:35.070 --> 00:15:37.543
And we will pick
the zones for you.

00:15:37.543 --> 00:15:39.210
Is there any reason
anybody need to pick

00:15:39.210 --> 00:15:40.950
a zone in your use case?

00:15:40.950 --> 00:15:46.140
Like specific zones
for a Redis instance?

00:15:46.140 --> 00:15:46.640
So yeah.

00:15:46.640 --> 00:15:48.265
So we recommend just
leaving it at any.

00:15:48.265 --> 00:15:50.210
And we will just pick
the zone for you.

00:15:50.210 --> 00:15:51.590
From a capacity
perspective, this

00:15:51.590 --> 00:15:53.750
is where essentially you
size your Redis instance.

00:15:53.750 --> 00:15:56.125
And as I mentioned, if you
look at the network throughput

00:15:56.125 --> 00:15:58.280
as you provision
more memory size,

00:15:58.280 --> 00:16:01.287
you get more network throughput.

00:16:01.287 --> 00:16:02.870
The other important
aspect to remember

00:16:02.870 --> 00:16:06.770
is the security model
for Cloud Memorystore.

00:16:06.770 --> 00:16:10.940
So today, the security
model we have essentially

00:16:10.940 --> 00:16:15.320
is that we only expose Cloud
Memorystore using private IP.

00:16:15.320 --> 00:16:18.440
And the other security
authorization that you can have

00:16:18.440 --> 00:16:23.090
is that you can restrict
the access to a specific VPC

00:16:23.090 --> 00:16:25.250
in your project.

00:16:25.250 --> 00:16:27.570
So that's the current
security model.

00:16:27.570 --> 00:16:29.790
We understand that
there are cases

00:16:29.790 --> 00:16:33.660
where you want to be able to
have much further restriction

00:16:33.660 --> 00:16:34.795
at the instance level.

00:16:34.795 --> 00:16:36.420
So that's something
that we are working

00:16:36.420 --> 00:16:38.400
on enabling, to
have instance level

00:16:38.400 --> 00:16:40.620
authentication for Memorystore.

00:16:40.620 --> 00:16:43.230
How many of you require
instance level auth?

00:16:43.230 --> 00:16:47.340
So the notion here is that
if you are authorizing

00:16:47.340 --> 00:16:50.910
just the VPC network, that
means pretty much any VM that

00:16:50.910 --> 00:16:53.110
is connected to that VPC
network and that project

00:16:53.110 --> 00:16:54.360
can access the Redis instance.

00:16:58.770 --> 00:16:59.830
So that's pretty much it.

00:16:59.830 --> 00:17:01.860
And if you go into
the advanced options,

00:17:01.860 --> 00:17:04.740
we have exposed a few of the
Redis configurations here.

00:17:04.740 --> 00:17:06.160
We haven't exposed all of them.

00:17:06.160 --> 00:17:07.800
And this is something
that we tried

00:17:07.800 --> 00:17:10.569
to do intentionally in terms
of exposing the config.

00:17:10.569 --> 00:17:12.510
But we exposed the main ones.

00:17:12.510 --> 00:17:14.579
The key one that you want
to just watch out for

00:17:14.579 --> 00:17:17.050
is max memory policy.

00:17:17.050 --> 00:17:18.839
The max memory
policy by default,

00:17:18.839 --> 00:17:20.550
we set it to volatile LRU.

00:17:20.550 --> 00:17:23.010
I'll talk more about the
eviction policies later on.

00:17:23.010 --> 00:17:24.660
But that's something
that you want

00:17:24.660 --> 00:17:27.790
to be super aware of in terms of
what is a default that we set.

00:17:27.790 --> 00:17:28.990
And that's volatile LRU.

00:17:32.850 --> 00:17:33.350
That's it.

00:17:33.350 --> 00:17:36.420
So you click create, oh shoot.

00:17:36.420 --> 00:17:38.730
I should use this.

00:17:38.730 --> 00:17:41.100
You click create, and
that's pretty much it.

00:17:41.100 --> 00:17:44.010
That's all you need to do to
provision a Redis instance.

00:17:46.640 --> 00:17:48.590
And once you provision,
you basically

00:17:48.590 --> 00:17:52.850
get the management capabilities
right here on the console.

00:17:52.850 --> 00:17:55.580
We have essentially
a list of metrics

00:17:55.580 --> 00:17:57.230
that'd be exposed in
the console itself

00:17:57.230 --> 00:17:59.420
that you can use to monitor.

00:17:59.420 --> 00:18:02.900
But the recommendation is
that you use Stackdriver

00:18:02.900 --> 00:18:04.790
for monitoring
Cloud Memorystore,

00:18:04.790 --> 00:18:07.490
because we exposed a lot
more metrics in Stackdriver

00:18:07.490 --> 00:18:09.470
compared to the console.

00:18:09.470 --> 00:18:11.870
And Stackdriver also gives
you additional capabilities

00:18:11.870 --> 00:18:14.130
like alerting, et cetera.

00:18:14.130 --> 00:18:16.560
So we will have a
demo in just a bit

00:18:16.560 --> 00:18:20.780
to just talk about the
different capabilities

00:18:20.780 --> 00:18:22.940
or different monitoring
metrics that we have,

00:18:22.940 --> 00:18:24.607
and what are some of
the things that you

00:18:24.607 --> 00:18:29.561
want to watch out for when
you monitor Memorystore.

00:18:32.930 --> 00:18:39.019
How many of you use Shared
VPC in your environment?

00:18:39.019 --> 00:18:39.938
Nobody?

00:18:39.938 --> 00:18:40.438
OK.

00:18:43.750 --> 00:18:45.460
The concept of Shared
VPC is something

00:18:45.460 --> 00:18:47.770
that you probably
will run into if you

00:18:47.770 --> 00:18:52.310
start using GCP a lot more.

00:18:52.310 --> 00:18:55.990
So the concept is that you can
basically share a VPC network

00:18:55.990 --> 00:18:57.910
across multiple projects.

00:18:57.910 --> 00:19:00.340
There's a concept of a host
project and service project,

00:19:00.340 --> 00:19:05.690
meaning if you are, let's say,
from a dev ops perspective,

00:19:05.690 --> 00:19:07.360
you wanna be able
to give autonomy

00:19:07.360 --> 00:19:08.860
to individual
application teams, you

00:19:08.860 --> 00:19:11.170
can basically create
multiple service projects

00:19:11.170 --> 00:19:14.280
and share this common
network across all of them.

00:19:14.280 --> 00:19:17.720
So in that particular case,
it's one of those things

00:19:17.720 --> 00:19:21.110
that if you want to deploy
Cloud Memorystore into one

00:19:21.110 --> 00:19:23.710
of those service projects, that
is currently not supported.

00:19:23.710 --> 00:19:25.710
That's one of the things
that we are working on.

00:19:25.710 --> 00:19:27.740
So if you have that
particular use case,

00:19:27.740 --> 00:19:30.330
that's something that we
will enable pretty quickly.

00:19:32.453 --> 00:19:34.370
KARTHI THYAGARAJAN: And
a good example of that

00:19:34.370 --> 00:19:35.300
I want to mention real quick.

00:19:35.300 --> 00:19:36.800
We have customers
who want to set up

00:19:36.800 --> 00:19:40.358
a demo environment, or sorry,
a dev or test environment,

00:19:40.358 --> 00:19:41.900
where they share
Memorystore instance

00:19:41.900 --> 00:19:44.678
across multiple applications,
or multiple versions

00:19:44.678 --> 00:19:45.470
of the application.

00:19:45.470 --> 00:19:49.828
A Shared VPC would be
useful in that case.

00:19:49.828 --> 00:19:51.870
GOPAL ASHOK: So one of
the other important points

00:19:51.870 --> 00:19:54.690
that I want to make with
where we are with Memorystore,

00:19:54.690 --> 00:19:57.930
is that both basic tier
and standard tier instances

00:19:57.930 --> 00:19:59.850
are single master.

00:19:59.850 --> 00:20:02.820
So that becomes an
interesting thing

00:20:02.820 --> 00:20:06.180
to be aware of,
because obviously Redis

00:20:06.180 --> 00:20:09.108
being single threaded,
there are certain thresholds

00:20:09.108 --> 00:20:10.900
that you will hit pretty
quickly if there's

00:20:10.900 --> 00:20:13.180
a single master based
on your workload.

00:20:13.180 --> 00:20:16.650
So the solution for that
obviously is Redis clustering.

00:20:16.650 --> 00:20:18.420
We haven't enabled
Redis clustering yet

00:20:18.420 --> 00:20:20.310
in Cloud Memorystore.

00:20:20.310 --> 00:20:22.720
So that's something, again,
that we are working on.

00:20:22.720 --> 00:20:24.930
So that's something that
you need to be aware of.

00:20:24.930 --> 00:20:28.908
And I've seen this a few
times where customers

00:20:28.908 --> 00:20:30.450
look at standard
tier and they expect

00:20:30.450 --> 00:20:34.650
it to be a scale out model.

00:20:34.650 --> 00:20:36.070
But in this
particular case, it's

00:20:36.070 --> 00:20:38.860
just a highly available Redis
instance with a single master.

00:20:42.230 --> 00:20:44.930
Has anybody deployed
Redis cluster today

00:20:44.930 --> 00:20:46.700
by yourself in GCP?

00:20:53.220 --> 00:20:56.400
So now what I want
to do is I want

00:20:56.400 --> 00:21:01.800
to touch up on some of the
best practices around when

00:21:01.800 --> 00:21:03.450
using Cloud Memorystore.

00:21:03.450 --> 00:21:04.920
So when I look at
best practices--

00:21:04.920 --> 00:21:09.960
and this kind of spans mostly
best practices around Redis--

00:21:09.960 --> 00:21:11.460
and there are some
specific things

00:21:11.460 --> 00:21:12.870
around Cloud
Memorystore that you

00:21:12.870 --> 00:21:16.080
want to be aware of when
you are using the service.

00:21:16.080 --> 00:21:19.880
So when you look at the
best practices around Redis,

00:21:19.880 --> 00:21:23.790
I see three, I look at it
as three buckets of things.

00:21:23.790 --> 00:21:25.850
One is, there are
things that you

00:21:25.850 --> 00:21:30.950
can do in terms of storing
data where you can do things

00:21:30.950 --> 00:21:33.570
to do things more efficiently.

00:21:33.570 --> 00:21:37.100
The other one is how do
you manage your evictions?

00:21:37.100 --> 00:21:41.060
And that kind of ties into
also the memory management

00:21:41.060 --> 00:21:42.940
aspects of Redis.

00:21:42.940 --> 00:21:46.060
And finally, monitoring,
that's something

00:21:46.060 --> 00:21:47.920
that we want to talk
about very specifically

00:21:47.920 --> 00:21:49.840
on Cloud Memorystore, because
there are certain things

00:21:49.840 --> 00:21:51.880
that we do from a managed
service perspective that

00:21:51.880 --> 00:21:53.440
will be useful for
you to understand,

00:21:53.440 --> 00:21:57.480
that will help you to manage
your instances better.

00:22:00.400 --> 00:22:01.900
From storing data
perspective, there

00:22:01.900 --> 00:22:04.530
are a lot of very good
recommendations out there.

00:22:04.530 --> 00:22:06.870
I just wanted to touch
upon like three things

00:22:06.870 --> 00:22:13.543
that we find very useful
from a Redis perspective.

00:22:13.543 --> 00:22:15.210
I think one of the
things is that if you

00:22:15.210 --> 00:22:17.980
can compress the data,
whenever possible, do that.

00:22:17.980 --> 00:22:21.600
So there are very good
libraries like Google

00:22:21.600 --> 00:22:26.610
has a snappy library that
you can use to compress data.

00:22:26.610 --> 00:22:28.830
So that's a very good
way where you can,

00:22:28.830 --> 00:22:31.380
depending on the kind of
data that you store in Redis,

00:22:31.380 --> 00:22:36.060
you can even get up
to 50% compression.

00:22:36.060 --> 00:22:38.310
So that's one of the things
that you should definitely

00:22:38.310 --> 00:22:42.680
look into when you are
using even Memorystore,

00:22:42.680 --> 00:22:45.800
because Memorystore behind the
scenes uses open source Redis.

00:22:45.800 --> 00:22:49.610
So any best practices
that applies

00:22:49.610 --> 00:22:54.770
to open source Redis also
applies to Cloud Memorystore.

00:22:54.770 --> 00:22:57.200
The other one that
we see quite a bit

00:22:57.200 --> 00:23:01.580
is that in terms of using
the right serializer.

00:23:01.580 --> 00:23:03.960
Java serializers are
notoriously inefficient.

00:23:03.960 --> 00:23:08.450
So there are some very
good serializers out there.

00:23:08.450 --> 00:23:10.280
Google's Protocol
Buffer is one way

00:23:10.280 --> 00:23:14.510
to serialize the data more
efficiently, even if you want

00:23:14.510 --> 00:23:16.500
it stored in a binary format.

00:23:16.500 --> 00:23:19.810
So that's one of
the things that you

00:23:19.810 --> 00:23:21.700
want to take a close
look at, again,

00:23:21.700 --> 00:23:23.350
in terms of how you store data.

00:23:23.350 --> 00:23:25.360
Because again, it's
all about how efficient

00:23:25.360 --> 00:23:28.190
you can be in terms of memory,
because memory is expensive.

00:23:28.190 --> 00:23:32.540
And the more data you store,
that can quickly add up.

00:23:32.540 --> 00:23:35.780
And I mentioned this before
using HASH data structure

00:23:35.780 --> 00:23:38.880
to reduce memory
usage is again, it all

00:23:38.880 --> 00:23:40.880
depends on the kind of
data that you're storing.

00:23:40.880 --> 00:23:42.980
But I think the key point
that I want to make here

00:23:42.980 --> 00:23:47.210
is that it's important for
you to look at it from a Redis

00:23:47.210 --> 00:23:50.210
perspective and apply the best
practices that you would apply

00:23:50.210 --> 00:23:52.820
for Redis if you were
deploying it on your own,

00:23:52.820 --> 00:23:55.400
even when you are using
Cloud Memorystore.

00:23:55.400 --> 00:23:57.110
So I think that's the
key point that I'm

00:23:57.110 --> 00:23:59.930
trying to make here when
it comes to best practices

00:23:59.930 --> 00:24:02.400
on storing data.

00:24:02.400 --> 00:24:05.430
Eviction policies are
very, very important.

00:24:05.430 --> 00:24:08.220
And it's something that you
have to be super aware of.

00:24:08.220 --> 00:24:13.080
And eviction policy is
all about how you actually

00:24:13.080 --> 00:24:16.110
want to store data, and how
long you want to store data,

00:24:16.110 --> 00:24:20.910
combined with how much money
you want to spend, in a way.

00:24:20.910 --> 00:24:23.430
So fundamentally, eviction
policies, as we all know,

00:24:23.430 --> 00:24:25.260
is essentially
the rules that you

00:24:25.260 --> 00:24:32.880
apply to evict data
from Redis memory

00:24:32.880 --> 00:24:35.790
based on certain
characteristics.

00:24:35.790 --> 00:24:39.200
So there are basically two
groups of eviction policies.

00:24:39.200 --> 00:24:40.715
There is the-- or
I would say three.

00:24:40.715 --> 00:24:42.090
There is no
eviction, which means

00:24:42.090 --> 00:24:45.180
that it's essentially
like a fixed memory size

00:24:45.180 --> 00:24:48.260
that once you hit
the memory limit,

00:24:48.260 --> 00:24:50.250
Redis is not going
to evict any data.

00:24:50.250 --> 00:24:53.580
And essentially Redis is
basically going to say,

00:24:53.580 --> 00:24:57.910
we can't store anymore
data in your instance.

00:24:57.910 --> 00:25:01.450
Then there's all keys
and volatile base LRUs.

00:25:01.450 --> 00:25:03.840
The volatile LRU like
I said, essentially is

00:25:03.840 --> 00:25:06.480
what we enable as a default.
And this is something

00:25:06.480 --> 00:25:07.920
that we recommend.

00:25:07.920 --> 00:25:11.130
It's an interesting
conversation when

00:25:11.130 --> 00:25:13.690
it comes to eviction policy.

00:25:13.690 --> 00:25:16.380
How many of you set TTLs
in your Redis instance?

00:25:19.782 --> 00:25:22.220
OK.

00:25:22.220 --> 00:25:24.140
So the interesting
thing about volatile LRU

00:25:24.140 --> 00:25:25.972
is that if you
don't set the TTL,

00:25:25.972 --> 00:25:27.680
then that essentially
means that there is

00:25:27.680 --> 00:25:30.740
no piece that can be evicted.

00:25:30.740 --> 00:25:34.710
So that's an interesting--

00:25:34.710 --> 00:25:37.810
But at the same
time, volatile LRU

00:25:37.810 --> 00:25:41.330
is a more efficient way
to manage your memory,

00:25:41.330 --> 00:25:46.270
because it essentially allows
you to set a TTL and evict,

00:25:46.270 --> 00:25:50.138
or at least expire the
keys once you hit the TTL.

00:25:50.138 --> 00:25:52.180
But that requires a little
bit more understanding

00:25:52.180 --> 00:25:55.720
of your usage patterns
and how much--

00:25:55.720 --> 00:25:57.490
what the keys are
used for, and how long

00:25:57.490 --> 00:25:59.650
you want the keys in
memory, et cetera.

00:25:59.650 --> 00:26:02.740
But that allows you to
basically be a little bit more

00:26:02.740 --> 00:26:07.730
efficient in terms of memory
and how large of an instance

00:26:07.730 --> 00:26:09.800
you want to provision.

00:26:09.800 --> 00:26:13.490
All keys LRU, on the other
hand, essentially what

00:26:13.490 --> 00:26:17.150
it says is that once
you hit the max memory,

00:26:17.150 --> 00:26:20.740
it's going to basically evict
the least recently used keys.

00:26:20.740 --> 00:26:23.900
But it basically is going
to fill up the memory

00:26:23.900 --> 00:26:26.260
and then start evicting keys.

00:26:26.260 --> 00:26:28.010
So there are some
interesting consequences

00:26:28.010 --> 00:26:31.467
of doing all keys LRUs
versus volatile LRU,

00:26:31.467 --> 00:26:36.340
and we'll talk about that in
our monitoring best practices.

00:26:36.340 --> 00:26:40.060
And Redis has recently
introduced an LFU policy,

00:26:40.060 --> 00:26:43.510
where it's a little bit more
efficient algorithm than LRU,

00:26:43.510 --> 00:26:45.730
where it's all
about how many times

00:26:45.730 --> 00:26:47.160
you have access to the keys.

00:26:47.160 --> 00:26:48.860
So LFU.

00:26:48.860 --> 00:26:53.700
So that's also an interesting--

00:26:53.700 --> 00:26:56.280
compared to a LRU, or
similar to LRU, again,

00:26:56.280 --> 00:26:58.950
you just need to have a
very good sense of how

00:26:58.950 --> 00:27:00.190
your keys are being used.

00:27:00.190 --> 00:27:02.880
And then using LRU
or LFU will actually

00:27:02.880 --> 00:27:06.250
help you with better
memory management in Redis.

00:27:06.250 --> 00:27:08.620
The other key thing
is, how many of you

00:27:08.620 --> 00:27:11.968
said you are using
Cloud Memorystore?

00:27:11.968 --> 00:27:14.010
Have you guys run into
memory fragmentation issue

00:27:14.010 --> 00:27:15.870
on your side?

00:27:15.870 --> 00:27:19.190
So we take care of that.

00:27:19.190 --> 00:27:22.040
So memory fragmentation is
a real problem in Redis.

00:27:22.040 --> 00:27:25.440
It's again, like I said, if you
are using open source Redis,

00:27:25.440 --> 00:27:27.920
you will run into memory
fragmentation problem.

00:27:27.920 --> 00:27:31.130
And what essentially, what
memory fragmentation is,

00:27:31.130 --> 00:27:35.805
it's basically Redis has
a concept of max memory.

00:27:35.805 --> 00:27:37.430
That is essentially
the memory that you

00:27:37.430 --> 00:27:40.970
set for the Redis process.

00:27:40.970 --> 00:27:44.480
But the way Redis
allocate memory,

00:27:44.480 --> 00:27:47.000
Redis can actually
consume more memory

00:27:47.000 --> 00:27:49.350
than what is allocated
for max memory.

00:27:49.350 --> 00:27:51.980
So it will essentially
creep into the system

00:27:51.980 --> 00:27:54.497
memory that you
provision, or the access

00:27:54.497 --> 00:27:55.580
memory that you provision.

00:27:55.580 --> 00:27:57.788
And at some point, you will
run out of system memory.

00:27:57.788 --> 00:27:59.630
And that will cause a crash.

00:27:59.630 --> 00:28:01.340
The reason memory
fragmentation happens

00:28:01.340 --> 00:28:02.940
is for a few different reasons.

00:28:02.940 --> 00:28:06.780
If you have very
homogeneous keys then

00:28:06.780 --> 00:28:10.160
the memory allocation
is pretty homogeneous

00:28:10.160 --> 00:28:13.880
when you use the
[INAUDIBLE] operator.

00:28:13.880 --> 00:28:15.710
But if you have
varying size keys,

00:28:15.710 --> 00:28:17.300
then it's quite
possible that you

00:28:17.300 --> 00:28:19.015
will run into a
high fragmentation,

00:28:19.015 --> 00:28:20.390
or there's more
chance of running

00:28:20.390 --> 00:28:22.042
into high fragmentation.

00:28:22.042 --> 00:28:23.500
So when high
fragmentation happens,

00:28:23.500 --> 00:28:25.400
there's a bunch of
memory that is unusable.

00:28:25.400 --> 00:28:27.530
And then we quickly
get into a situation

00:28:27.530 --> 00:28:30.860
where Redis has to consume
more memory than what

00:28:30.860 --> 00:28:32.000
is allocated for Redis.

00:28:32.000 --> 00:28:34.532
And then you get into that
issue of basically running out

00:28:34.532 --> 00:28:35.240
of system memory.

00:28:35.240 --> 00:28:36.782
And that can
potentially crash Redis.

00:28:39.318 --> 00:28:41.610
So one of the key things that
you want to watch out for

00:28:41.610 --> 00:28:47.300
is have things in
place that will

00:28:47.300 --> 00:28:49.340
allow you to detect
memory fragmentation

00:28:49.340 --> 00:28:50.400
and take action on it.

00:28:50.400 --> 00:28:52.790
So our best practices
demo, we will

00:28:52.790 --> 00:28:54.968
walk through what is that
you need to watch out for,

00:28:54.968 --> 00:28:56.510
because there are
only certain things

00:28:56.510 --> 00:28:59.857
that we can do from a
managed service perspective.

00:28:59.857 --> 00:29:01.940
There are things that we
do from a managed service

00:29:01.940 --> 00:29:05.130
perspective to ensure that your
systems are up and running.

00:29:05.130 --> 00:29:06.680
But there are also
cases where you

00:29:06.680 --> 00:29:09.425
want to be proactive about
not getting into a situation

00:29:09.425 --> 00:29:12.940
where you have high
memory fragmentation.

00:29:12.940 --> 00:29:15.190
This problem was
actually worse in 3.2,

00:29:15.190 --> 00:29:19.420
but in 4.0 they have introduced
active de-fragmentation

00:29:19.420 --> 00:29:20.980
so that when the
instance is running,

00:29:20.980 --> 00:29:25.300
there's a background thread that
basically de-frags the memory.

00:29:25.300 --> 00:29:27.725
This comes with a
cost of a higher CPU.

00:29:27.725 --> 00:29:29.350
So this is something
that you basically

00:29:29.350 --> 00:29:32.320
want to turn on and see
for your workload what

00:29:32.320 --> 00:29:34.300
the CPU overhead is.

00:29:34.300 --> 00:29:36.017
But based on
everything that we have

00:29:36.017 --> 00:29:38.350
seen in the community and the
testing that we have done,

00:29:38.350 --> 00:29:40.210
it seems to be a safe
thing to turn on.

00:29:40.210 --> 00:29:42.500
But again, depending
on your workload

00:29:42.500 --> 00:29:46.820
you want to monitor your
CPU when you do this.

00:29:46.820 --> 00:29:50.810
So monitoring fragmentation
and taking action

00:29:50.810 --> 00:29:54.440
is one of the things that if
you start using Memorystore,

00:29:54.440 --> 00:29:57.100
you absolutely should
know how to do that.

00:29:57.100 --> 00:29:58.850
And even if you are
not using Memorystore,

00:29:58.850 --> 00:30:02.108
from a Redis perspective, it
is important to understand

00:30:02.108 --> 00:30:03.650
what are some of
the metrics that you

00:30:03.650 --> 00:30:07.670
should be monitoring to make
sure that your system runs

00:30:07.670 --> 00:30:10.630
in a very stable manner.

00:30:10.630 --> 00:30:12.370
So let's go over that.

00:30:15.224 --> 00:30:17.224
KARTHI THYAGARAJAN: Just
switch over real quick.

00:30:17.224 --> 00:30:18.277
GOPAL ASHOK: Oh sorry.

00:30:18.277 --> 00:30:19.694
KARTHI THYAGARAJAN:
It's all good.

00:30:23.650 --> 00:30:27.010
GOPAL ASHOK: So let me quickly
walk through the Stackdriver

00:30:27.010 --> 00:30:27.610
monitoring.

00:30:27.610 --> 00:30:33.470
If you haven't used
Stackdriver before,

00:30:33.470 --> 00:30:38.070
so Stackdriver is Google
Cloud's monitoring platform.

00:30:38.070 --> 00:30:39.840
So it's like Cloud
Launch for EWS,

00:30:39.840 --> 00:30:42.030
if you're familiar with a EWS.

00:30:42.030 --> 00:30:44.970
So in Stackdriver,
what he can do

00:30:44.970 --> 00:30:49.460
is you can essentially
go look for metrics.

00:30:49.460 --> 00:30:52.170
So in this particular
case, you can say Redis.

00:30:57.920 --> 00:31:01.980
And we have exposed a lot of
metrics related to our Redis

00:31:01.980 --> 00:31:02.520
over here.

00:31:04.818 --> 00:31:06.860
So there's a lot of metrics
that you can monitor.

00:31:06.860 --> 00:31:09.992
But the question is, what are
the right metrics to monitor.

00:31:09.992 --> 00:31:12.200
The baseline set of metrics
that you want to monitor,

00:31:12.200 --> 00:31:14.825
and what are some of the trends
that you want to watch out for.

00:31:14.825 --> 00:31:18.030
And that's what we want
to talk about here.

00:31:18.030 --> 00:31:21.810
So what we have done is we have
created a couple of dashboards

00:31:21.810 --> 00:31:22.350
here.

00:31:22.350 --> 00:31:25.490
Let me just go back.

00:31:25.490 --> 00:31:27.045
So what we have
here is an instance

00:31:27.045 --> 00:31:29.170
where we are running a
benchmark behind the scenes.

00:31:29.170 --> 00:31:31.870
We are running a
[INAUDIBLE] benchmark.

00:31:31.870 --> 00:31:35.500
So what we are
essentially doing is,

00:31:35.500 --> 00:31:38.770
we have created an instance
with the basic default eviction

00:31:38.770 --> 00:31:41.110
policy, which is volatile LRU.

00:31:41.110 --> 00:31:44.890
And we have set the time
to live to be very short.

00:31:44.890 --> 00:31:48.650
So in this particular
case, as you can see,

00:31:48.650 --> 00:31:50.355
the max memory is set to 10 GB.

00:31:50.355 --> 00:31:51.730
So the first graph
is essentially

00:31:51.730 --> 00:31:54.092
a graph with the max
memory and use memory.

00:31:54.092 --> 00:31:56.050
So the system is actually
running pretty stable

00:31:56.050 --> 00:31:57.130
right now.

00:31:57.130 --> 00:32:01.030
So there is enough memory
available for Redis to consume.

00:32:01.030 --> 00:32:02.560
But that's one
graph that you want

00:32:02.560 --> 00:32:08.770
to monitor to ensure that
your memory consumption is OK.

00:32:08.770 --> 00:32:11.290
The next graph, which is the
system memory usage ratio

00:32:11.290 --> 00:32:14.630
is something that
we just released.

00:32:14.630 --> 00:32:16.360
So this is essentially
the graph that

00:32:16.360 --> 00:32:20.430
tells you how much fragmentation
do you have in your system.

00:32:20.430 --> 00:32:22.800
So system memory
usage ratio, what

00:32:22.800 --> 00:32:25.950
we do when we manage services is
that Redis obviously requires--

00:32:25.950 --> 00:32:27.450
when you provision
a Redis instance,

00:32:27.450 --> 00:32:29.910
you obviously require
some overhead to take

00:32:29.910 --> 00:32:32.360
care of known data set.

00:32:32.360 --> 00:32:34.710
And Redis a whole bunch
of background threads

00:32:34.710 --> 00:32:37.180
and operations that they do.

00:32:37.180 --> 00:32:40.800
So there is a recommendation
of, you need to provision

00:32:40.800 --> 00:32:42.600
some system memory
over max memory

00:32:42.600 --> 00:32:44.520
to make sure that
the system is up

00:32:44.520 --> 00:32:48.670
and running in a single fashion
so we take care of that today.

00:32:48.670 --> 00:32:50.700
But the thing is that
you need to be able to--

00:32:50.700 --> 00:32:53.010
even then, there
will be fragmentation

00:32:53.010 --> 00:32:54.910
that happens in your system.

00:32:54.910 --> 00:32:57.030
So when you monitor
system memory usage ratio,

00:32:57.030 --> 00:32:58.530
essentially what
you are looking for

00:32:58.530 --> 00:33:00.930
is you want to look for
whether the graph is

00:33:00.930 --> 00:33:03.930
trending towards 100%.

00:33:03.930 --> 00:33:07.770
So based on the overhead that
we provision behind the scenes,

00:33:07.770 --> 00:33:09.660
your system use and
memory usage ratio

00:33:09.660 --> 00:33:11.970
will always start
at a percentage.

00:33:11.970 --> 00:33:15.840
Typically it's going to be
around 60%, which is fine.

00:33:15.840 --> 00:33:18.480
Because that's based on
the amount of overhead

00:33:18.480 --> 00:33:20.050
that we provision
behind the scenes.

00:33:20.050 --> 00:33:22.050
But the key thing that
you want to watch out for

00:33:22.050 --> 00:33:24.630
is what is a trend as
your workload peaks

00:33:24.630 --> 00:33:27.420
and over a period of time,
whether it's trending towards,

00:33:27.420 --> 00:33:30.330
let's say 70%, 80%, 90%.

00:33:30.330 --> 00:33:32.630
So that's a key graph to watch.

00:33:32.630 --> 00:33:34.208
Bytes pending
replication, again,

00:33:34.208 --> 00:33:36.750
is a very interesting thing that
you should always watch for,

00:33:36.750 --> 00:33:40.645
because Redis replication
is asynchronous.

00:33:40.645 --> 00:33:42.020
And depending on
your right load,

00:33:42.020 --> 00:33:44.000
you can obviously have a--

00:33:44.000 --> 00:33:47.120
depending on the right load,
and the kind of instance

00:33:47.120 --> 00:33:52.140
that you have, you can have a
pretty big replication backlog.

00:33:52.140 --> 00:33:54.140
And this is just
the nature of Redis.

00:33:54.140 --> 00:33:58.130
So what that tells you is
whether your instance is

00:33:58.130 --> 00:34:00.800
able to sustain the workload
that you have running

00:34:00.800 --> 00:34:02.540
on that particular instance.

00:34:02.540 --> 00:34:05.360
The consequence of bytes
pending replication going high

00:34:05.360 --> 00:34:08.060
essentially is that it can
also drive higher memory

00:34:08.060 --> 00:34:08.810
consumption.

00:34:08.810 --> 00:34:11.690
But also Redis can
get into this behavior

00:34:11.690 --> 00:34:14.060
of doing a full synchronization
once the bytes pending

00:34:14.060 --> 00:34:17.639
replication go below
a certain limit.

00:34:17.639 --> 00:34:19.681
So that's again
another key graph

00:34:19.681 --> 00:34:22.909
to watch out for
when you are doing,

00:34:22.909 --> 00:34:25.010
just managing Redis in general.

00:34:25.010 --> 00:34:27.280
But all these metrics
are exposed for you.

00:34:27.280 --> 00:34:30.350
So you can just use
Stackdriver to do that.

00:34:30.350 --> 00:34:32.840
The other interesting
one is keys, the number

00:34:32.840 --> 00:34:35.179
of keys versus expirable keys.

00:34:35.179 --> 00:34:39.420
It's a good thing to understand
in terms of, hey first of all,

00:34:39.420 --> 00:34:41.179
if you're using
volatile LRU and if you

00:34:41.179 --> 00:34:44.150
don't see expirable
keys that's a bad sign.

00:34:44.150 --> 00:34:46.190
That means you haven't
really set TTL,

00:34:46.190 --> 00:34:48.050
and you either should
set a TTL or you

00:34:48.050 --> 00:34:52.068
should change your eviction
policy to all keys LRU.

00:34:57.238 --> 00:34:59.280
KARTHI THYAGARAJAN: Can
you scroll down, actually

00:34:59.280 --> 00:34:59.780
scroll down.

00:35:01.735 --> 00:35:03.360
GOPAL ASHOK: A few
more things that you

00:35:03.360 --> 00:35:05.420
want to watch out
for is, obviously

00:35:05.420 --> 00:35:09.230
CPU network throughput is a
good one to just keep an eye on.

00:35:09.230 --> 00:35:11.380
And evicted keys,
you can understand.

00:35:11.380 --> 00:35:13.650
So for example, if you
run into memory pressure,

00:35:13.650 --> 00:35:15.192
and if you're not
seeing an eviction,

00:35:15.192 --> 00:35:18.830
that's something that you
want to go take a look at.

00:35:18.830 --> 00:35:21.650
The failover one is
also an interesting one,

00:35:21.650 --> 00:35:24.650
because there are
scenarios where

00:35:24.650 --> 00:35:26.900
we have to failover the
instance without you knowing

00:35:26.900 --> 00:35:28.983
or triggering just to make
sure that the system is

00:35:28.983 --> 00:35:30.280
in a stable state.

00:35:30.280 --> 00:35:33.200
So it's very good to know
whether if multiple failovers

00:35:33.200 --> 00:35:35.010
happen in a period of time.

00:35:35.010 --> 00:35:36.850
And that can also
have consequences

00:35:36.850 --> 00:35:39.500
to your application because
when we do a failover, obviously

00:35:39.500 --> 00:35:43.470
we disconnect the clients.

00:35:43.470 --> 00:35:45.950
So if you are running into
behaviors or scenarios

00:35:45.950 --> 00:35:48.440
where you're like,
why is my application

00:35:48.440 --> 00:35:49.972
getting disconnected,
or if there

00:35:49.972 --> 00:35:51.680
is some kind of
inconsistency when you're

00:35:51.680 --> 00:35:53.980
running a job, for example.

00:35:53.980 --> 00:35:56.690
If you do a failover,
because Redis replication is

00:35:56.690 --> 00:36:00.920
asynchronous, there could
be stale data or potentially

00:36:00.920 --> 00:36:02.900
loss of data when that happens.

00:36:02.900 --> 00:36:06.320
So monitoring if failovers are
happening in your environment

00:36:06.320 --> 00:36:09.350
is a good way to understand
if some of the application

00:36:09.350 --> 00:36:12.500
behavior that is happening
is due to the failover that's

00:36:12.500 --> 00:36:13.890
happening in the system.

00:36:13.890 --> 00:36:17.000
If you see a lot of failovers,
in Cloud Memorystore, that

00:36:17.000 --> 00:36:19.020
means there's
something going on.

00:36:19.020 --> 00:36:21.990
And we will know about
it, we'll take care of it.

00:36:21.990 --> 00:36:25.620
But it's important for you also
to know that that's going on.

00:36:25.620 --> 00:36:28.850
So these I would say
are the key metrics

00:36:28.850 --> 00:36:31.560
that you should be
monitoring regardless

00:36:31.560 --> 00:36:34.010
of whether things that are
bad or happening or not.

00:36:34.010 --> 00:36:36.140
When you provision
Memorystore, you

00:36:36.140 --> 00:36:39.888
should be just monitoring these
metrics in your environment.

00:36:39.888 --> 00:36:43.360
Let's take a quick look
out of their graph,

00:36:43.360 --> 00:36:45.250
same set of metrics.

00:36:45.250 --> 00:36:48.370
But the interesting thing here
is that we ran this benchmark

00:36:48.370 --> 00:36:49.715
and we set a long TTL.

00:36:49.715 --> 00:36:51.340
And that's essentially
what I'm talking

00:36:51.340 --> 00:36:53.920
about in terms of the
system memory usage ratio

00:36:53.920 --> 00:36:57.100
keep going up.

00:36:57.100 --> 00:36:59.060
I think we saw the
benchmark at some point.

00:36:59.060 --> 00:37:01.690
But if you continue to run that
benchmark for the instance size

00:37:01.690 --> 00:37:03.065
that we had, we
essentially would

00:37:03.065 --> 00:37:07.400
have hit the system memory usage
ratio of 100%, at that point,

00:37:07.400 --> 00:37:09.070
we will failover.

00:37:09.070 --> 00:37:11.390
And we will try to keep
the system up and running.

00:37:11.390 --> 00:37:13.510
But in some cases,
it can get extreme

00:37:13.510 --> 00:37:15.970
where we are not able
to, or the system

00:37:15.970 --> 00:37:18.230
is not able to recover itself.

00:37:18.230 --> 00:37:19.630
So that's the
reason we recommend

00:37:19.630 --> 00:37:21.790
that you monitor your
system memory usage

00:37:21.790 --> 00:37:24.010
ratio to be around 80%.

00:37:24.010 --> 00:37:26.170
Set an alert in Stackdriver.

00:37:26.170 --> 00:37:29.500
And the action that you can
take at that point, essentially

00:37:29.500 --> 00:37:31.510
is to scale your instance.

00:37:31.510 --> 00:37:33.250
So that will
essentially give you

00:37:33.250 --> 00:37:36.260
more memory for the workload
that you're running.

00:37:36.260 --> 00:37:41.020
So that's a very important
graph to keep an eye on and also

00:37:41.020 --> 00:37:44.380
put some alerts on.

00:37:44.380 --> 00:37:47.120
So I talked about monitoring
system memory usage ratio

00:37:47.120 --> 00:37:49.790
and then triggering an alert.

00:37:49.790 --> 00:37:51.620
The question is,
and I also said, OK,

00:37:51.620 --> 00:37:53.130
do you want to do scaling?

00:37:53.130 --> 00:37:54.890
So one of the--

00:37:54.890 --> 00:37:57.860
so the question is, what is
an efficient way to do it.

00:37:57.860 --> 00:37:59.402
Karthi, do you want
to talk about it?

00:37:59.402 --> 00:38:01.070
KARTHI THYAGARAJAN: Sure.

00:38:01.070 --> 00:38:04.940
So as we were kind of
working through this demo,

00:38:04.940 --> 00:38:07.040
one of the things we
wanted to showcase

00:38:07.040 --> 00:38:10.040
is the fact that in
addition to the reliability

00:38:10.040 --> 00:38:12.120
that you get with Cloud
Memorystore for Redis,

00:38:12.120 --> 00:38:14.630
as opposed to running
Redis on your own

00:38:14.630 --> 00:38:20.100
is the GCP ecosystem of
services that you get to use.

00:38:20.100 --> 00:38:24.140
And this is a good example of
that where as Gopal mentioned,

00:38:24.140 --> 00:38:28.070
you look at these trends,
for example, system memory

00:38:28.070 --> 00:38:30.800
ratio, and other things.

00:38:30.800 --> 00:38:35.340
As they trend in a direction,
you want to take action.

00:38:35.340 --> 00:38:40.070
You could take action manually,
or you could automate it.

00:38:40.070 --> 00:38:42.540
And this is one way to do it.

00:38:42.540 --> 00:38:44.750
The reason we want to talk
about doing it this way

00:38:44.750 --> 00:38:46.160
is because it's
fully serverless.

00:38:46.160 --> 00:38:47.452
And serverless is all the rage.

00:38:47.452 --> 00:38:50.540
You're not standing up separate
VMs and things like that.

00:38:50.540 --> 00:38:53.930
And this counts on the fact
that Stackdriver is essentially

00:38:53.930 --> 00:38:56.420
a giant time series database.

00:38:56.420 --> 00:38:59.210
So you can inspect the
metrics that you're

00:38:59.210 --> 00:39:01.580
interested in inspecting,
for example, the ones

00:39:01.580 --> 00:39:03.200
that Gopal pointed to.

00:39:03.200 --> 00:39:07.180
And you can do that
on a scheduled basis.

00:39:07.180 --> 00:39:09.320
As I'll show you,
we're going to do it

00:39:09.320 --> 00:39:12.110
once every minute,
sample the metrics

00:39:12.110 --> 00:39:14.690
that we're interested in kind
of seeing the trends for.

00:39:14.690 --> 00:39:17.210
And then based on
how they're trending,

00:39:17.210 --> 00:39:22.340
you can actually use a Cloud
function to scale up your Cloud

00:39:22.340 --> 00:39:24.080
Memorystore for Redis instance.

00:39:24.080 --> 00:39:26.960
And this is because
we give you an API.

00:39:26.960 --> 00:39:30.650
Just like with every
service that GCP has,

00:39:30.650 --> 00:39:33.680
Cloud Memorystore has an
API to initiate a scale up.

00:39:33.680 --> 00:39:36.030
So you can automate this.

00:39:36.030 --> 00:39:37.650
So let's look at
that real quick.

00:39:37.650 --> 00:39:39.150
If we could switch
back to the demo.

00:39:41.710 --> 00:39:44.960
So I mentioned the
Cloud Scheduler service.

00:39:44.960 --> 00:39:47.813
This is a service that
we recently released.

00:39:47.813 --> 00:39:50.230
You don't want to stand up a
VM and run [INAUDIBLE] on it.

00:39:50.230 --> 00:39:53.170
You want to use a
reliable service for this.

00:39:53.170 --> 00:39:58.800
The way this works is you
can set up this service

00:39:58.800 --> 00:40:00.780
to generate a Pub/Sub message.

00:40:00.780 --> 00:40:03.340
And then in this case, we're
going to do it once a minute.

00:40:03.340 --> 00:40:05.310
And then based on the
Pub/Sub message getting

00:40:05.310 --> 00:40:08.707
deposited into a topic, you
could run a Cloud Function.

00:40:08.707 --> 00:40:10.790
And I'll quickly talk
through this Cloud Function.

00:40:10.790 --> 00:40:12.880
I don't want to take
up too much time.

00:40:12.880 --> 00:40:14.510
I hope there are folks who--

00:40:14.510 --> 00:40:17.453
or most of the folks in this
room hopefully know Python.

00:40:17.453 --> 00:40:18.870
Essentially what
we're going to do

00:40:18.870 --> 00:40:25.440
is check to see if our
system memory usage

00:40:25.440 --> 00:40:27.300
ratio is trending up rapidly.

00:40:27.300 --> 00:40:29.550
And that is up to you to decide.

00:40:29.550 --> 00:40:32.100
And then another thing we
have to pay attention to,

00:40:32.100 --> 00:40:37.080
if you remember, we were
tracking the bytes pending

00:40:37.080 --> 00:40:38.070
replication.

00:40:38.070 --> 00:40:40.710
We want to make sure that this
is not some crazy number where

00:40:40.710 --> 00:40:42.750
it's like 8 gigs behind.

00:40:42.750 --> 00:40:47.610
You don't want to trigger a
scale up event at that time,

00:40:47.610 --> 00:40:49.563
because you might
have data loss.

00:40:49.563 --> 00:40:50.730
So you have to balance this.

00:40:50.730 --> 00:40:53.550
And what we would
recommend is, try

00:40:53.550 --> 00:40:55.470
to get ahead of the
situation, where

00:40:55.470 --> 00:40:58.120
if you're seeing
growth early on,

00:40:58.120 --> 00:41:00.120
and you know the growth
is going to continue on,

00:41:00.120 --> 00:41:04.770
then trigger this kind
of scale up event.

00:41:04.770 --> 00:41:09.220
And then at that point you
decide how much to scale up by.

00:41:09.220 --> 00:41:11.530
In this case I just
randomly picked two.

00:41:11.530 --> 00:41:14.390
And you call the
scale up function,

00:41:14.390 --> 00:41:19.690
which uses the Cloud Memorystore
for Redis Python SDK,

00:41:19.690 --> 00:41:26.530
and basically sets the memory
size to the new value that you

00:41:26.530 --> 00:41:27.790
would like it to have.

00:41:27.790 --> 00:41:30.400
Of course, you want to
make sure that you do this

00:41:30.400 --> 00:41:34.300
with some boundaries in
mind, because obviously we

00:41:34.300 --> 00:41:35.860
have a limit of 300 gigs.

00:41:35.860 --> 00:41:39.100
And you may have some limits
in terms of budgets and things

00:41:39.100 --> 00:41:42.460
like that, keep that in
mind when you automate it.

00:41:42.460 --> 00:41:46.512
But it is recommended that you
automate this within reason.

00:41:46.512 --> 00:41:47.470
GOPAL ASHOK: All right.

00:41:47.470 --> 00:41:48.105
Thank you very much.

00:41:48.105 --> 00:41:49.800
It was a pleasure
talking to you all.

00:41:49.800 --> 00:41:53.450
[MUSIC PLAYING]

